# What this port was made from, and how to notice when it moves.
#
# Source:  dsh-crew 0.9.0  (https://github.com/stuarthu/dsh-crew)
# Tag:     v0.9.0
# Commit:  7bc7181
#
# Every line below is the SHA-256 of a dsh-crew file AS COMMITTED at that tag —
# the state this port was made from. One line per ported file: the ten role
# prompts, the five host/*.js files whose rules this port carries or skips,
# principles.md and README.md. Seventeen lines. The last comment block in this
# file says what is left out and why, and porting.md says why not more.
#
# The count went from fifteen to seventeen at 0.9.0: upstream added
# roles/test-engineer.md and roles/code-engineer.md, the two halves of the
# paired shape.
#
# To see what changed since, clone the newest TAG into a throwaway folder:
#
#   TMP=$(mktemp -d)
#   git clone --quiet https://github.com/stuarthu/dsh-crew "$TMP/dsh-crew"
#   git -C "$TMP/dsh-crew" checkout --quiet <the newest tag>
#   git -C "$TMP/dsh-crew" status          # read the answer before you trust it
#   cd "$TMP/dsh-crew" && sha256sum -c ~/workspace/claude-crew/upstream.sums
#   cd "$TMP/dsh-crew" && shasum -a 256 -c ~/workspace/claude-crew/upstream.sums   # macOS
#
# A tag, not `main`, and not somebody's working copy: a tag is a decision a
# person made, and this port's 0.2.0 pass compared against a mid-flight commit,
# which is how the gap grew to 11,000 lines before anyone measured it.
# NEVER read, write or run anything in ~/workspace/dsh-crew. That is the user's
# own working copy, it is usually half-finished, and a sum taken from it is
# worthless. `sha256sum -c` compares the WORKING TREE of whatever checkout you
# stand in, so a fresh clone at a tag is the only state that means anything; if
# you reuse a checkout anyway, run `git status` in it first and read the answer.
#
# A line reported as FAILED means that dsh-crew file changed. The comment above
# it says which claude-crew file to look at, and why. porting.md holds the full
# map and the steps of a port pass.
#
# WHAT A FAILED LINE MEANS. Exactly one thing: upstream moved, go and read the
# diff. That was not true at 0.3.0, when ten paragraphs here deliberately said
# something different from upstream and nine of the fifteen lines below carried
# the word DIVERGENCE. Upstream v0.9.0 reached the same answer on all ten —
# eight of them in almost this port's own words — and on the last two the user
# chose upstream's shape. So porting.md's divergence table is EMPTY, no line
# below carries a divergence warning, and a pass has one job again: carry the
# change across.
#
# Open porting.md's divergence table anyway, before you read a diff. It takes
# ten seconds, and a later pass may have added a row with the user's yes. A
# pass may NOT add a row on its own: it reports what it found in the hand-off
# file and stops. porting.md's rule at the top says why — a port carries the
# mechanism and never the fix.
#
# THE SHARED WORDING. THREE blocks are carried word for word into all nine
# agents/*.md and the skill: Rule A, on text arriving inside a tool result;
# Rule B, on the documents that judge a role's work; and the one line that closes
# the write-set statement, **Reading is not restricted, and you should read widely.**
# Check the third with:
#   grep -c '^\*\*Reading is not restricted, and you should read widely\.\*\*$' <file>
# expecting 1 in each of the nine.
#
# principles.md holds the authoritative copy of all three. Changing any one of them
# means changing all ten files in the
# same commit, and checking it with the recipe written beside that block —
# INCLUDING its `test -s` line, because a range that matches nothing yields a
# stable hash of the empty string, and seven files with the rule missing then
# produce seven identical "passing" hashes. That was measured here.
#
# After carrying a change across, replace that line with the new sum:
#   cd "$TMP/dsh-crew" && sha256sum roles/pm.md
# and update the Source, Tag and Commit lines above when a whole pass is done.
#
# `sha256sum -c` cannot report a file it has never heard of, so a NEW upstream
# role, or a rule moved into a new file, is invisible here. porting.md carries
# the by-hand check for that — and it is not theoretical: this pass found two
# new role files that way.

