# WHAT IS NOT PINNED, AND WHY.
#
# Upstream v0.9.0 carries 335 files under its docs/ that this file has never
# named, and after reading them the answer is still no:
#
#   docs/decisions/  — upstream's own ADRs and CRDs (50 files at this tag);
#   docs/design/     — upstream's own PRDs, designs and task table (5);
#   docs/qa/         — upstream's QA cases, its run-all.sh and its qa lib (278);
#   docs/research/   — upstream's own research answers (2).
#
# Every one of them is upstream's record of upstream's own jobs. None holds a rule
# this port carries, skips or restates, which is the test a pinned line has to
# pass. Pinning them would report dozens of FAILED lines per pass that say nothing
# about this port, and a check nobody reads is worse than no check, because it
# looks like one.
#
# The same goes for package.json, tools/, preset/, .github/workflows/ and
# cordis.patch.yml: nothing there is ported, and porting.md's "did not port"
# table names each of them with its reason.
#
# Two of those unpinned folders are still worth reading by hand when a question
# comes up. docs/research/document-types.md holds the full quotations behind
# principles.md's "What each kind of document holds", including the sources that
# could not be reached. And docs/qa/ is where upstream's own checks live: this
# port has none, by design rule 6, so upstream's cases are the only place to see
# what a rule was meant to make true. That is a person's job, not a checksum's.
